+- `TF_FLASHNEXT_TP_PROMPT_ASYNC=1` (rank-local) queues a non-final prompt chunk at `--tp 2` without draining it on
+  either rank or waiting for rank 1's ack after it; rank 0 reads those acks, in order, with its next one. Same launches
+  in the same order, so the same bytes. `tf-cuda-test flashnext-async-rank` compares them with drained chunks.
+- `TF_FLASHNEXT_MIXED_ROUNDS=1` (both ranks, drafting, `TF_FLASHNEXT_TP_MOE_PARTIAL=1`) lets a long prompt's middle
+  piece of `TF_FLASHNEXT_MIXED_ROWS` rows (multiples of 64, default 256) wait for the next decode round and run in its
+  forward. Each group keeps its own pass, scratch and kernels except the MoE block, which runs once a layer over both
+  groups' rows, so the round reads the expert weights once instead of once for the piece and again for the round. A
+  row's MoE arithmetic is its own (router, top-k, the native shared expert, NVFP4 tiles with the decode bytes, the
+  ranks' fp32 add), so every row keeps its bytes. A prompt's last piece, a piece ending at a cache mark, and image rows
+  run alone, as before. `tf-cuda-test flashnext-mixed-rank` compares a mixed round with the piece's pass followed by
+  the shared verify, byte for byte. EXL3 experts (`TF_FLASHNEXT_EXPERTS_EXL3`) pick their kernels by a pass's rows
+  (`TF_EXL3_PROMPT_ROWS`, 256 unset): a group keeps its bytes only when its own pass and the round take the same
+  kernels. With 84-row windows a 128-row piece keeps every row's bytes; from 256 rows the windows' rows take the prompt
+  kernels' bytes. `TF_EXL3_PROMPT_ROWS=1` puts every pass on the prompt kernels, and every row keeps its bytes again.
+  `TF_FLASHNEXT_MIXED_MS=X` (rank 0, 1 to 10000; unset or 0: off) sizes each piece from the last mixed rounds' times
+  so a round's forward stays under X ms: it learns the time a piece row adds from two rounds of different rows, drops
+  the rows a round overran by, and adds 64 while the next 64 should still fit, between 64 and the chunk's room beside
+  the widest round.
 
 ## Experimental K32 prompt experts
 
@@ -73,6 +91,15 @@ prompt-cache budget fits it. The first pass is unchanged; a second identical pas
 resume there. Chat endpoints retain priority. `TF_FLASHNEXT_REPEAT_CACHE=0` disables this Flash Next CUDA policy.
 Other families keep their existing cache policy. Full token keys and frame identity still decide every resume.
 
+`TENSORFOLD_PCACHE_SHARED_MIN=<tokens>` (native CUDA families; unset or `0`: off) keeps one more state when a prompt
+shares at least that many tokens with a kept prompt: the state one token before they part, flagged shared, so a new
+conversation that shares an agent harness's instructions resumes there instead of at token 0. Without it a first
+turn's kept states (the history's end, the rewind state, the system block's cuts) all lie past the part when prompts
+differ inside the system block, and a hybrid model can only resume at a kept position. A prompt keeps at most one
+such state, none within 256 tokens of where it resumes, so later conversations resume one state rather than copying it.
+Resuming is exact: replies do not change. Each state costs the family's fixed state plus its per-token rows
+(Flash Next: about 55 MiB plus 6.8 KB a token on rank 0). Start with 4096.
+
